--TEST--
`as string` over a `?bytes` or a `mixed` holding `bytes` validates UTF-8 exactly as `bytes as string` does, agrees with `as ?string`, and `.` still refuses the same value — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:expressions/nullable-conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The static `bytes as string` is the checked row. A union only knows its tag
// at run time, and the written conversion must take the same row from there:
// a `?bytes` read off a stream is the ordinary way a program meets this.
bytes $plain = "text" as bytes;
?bytes $maybe = "text" as bytes;
mixed $any = "text" as bytes;
?bytes $broken = "\u{e9}" as bytes;
$broken = Core\Bytes::slice($broken as bytes, 0, 1);

echo $plain as string, "\n";
echo $maybe as string, "\n";
echo $any as string, "\n";

// Agreement, counted: the three spellings of the checked row, and the
// nullable twin, answer one string for one value.
int $agree = 0;
if (($maybe as string) == ($plain as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (($any as string) == ($plain as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (($maybe as ?string ?? "<null>") == ($maybe as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (($any as ?string ?? "<null>") == ($any as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
echo $agree, "\n";

// The failing side of the same row: one byte of a two-byte sequence is not
// UTF-8. The checked spelling throws where the nullable one answers `null`.
echo $broken as ?string ?? "<null>", "\n";
try {
    echo $broken as string, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "caught: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// A `null` operand renders as the empty string, as it does from every `?T`.
?bytes $none = null;
echo "[", $none as string, "]\n";

// `.` converts nothing it was not asked to, so the same union is refused there.
try {
    echo "joined: " . $any, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "caught: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
text
text
text
4
<null>
caught: cannot convert `bytes` to `string`: not well-formed UTF-8 at byte 0
[]
caught: cannot convert a `bytes` value to `string`
